{"authored" [{"question" "q-72175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le y\\le 2}.$\n\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-72175/review.json" "file_sha256" "11e3ab7de29c35db8fbaa197e2dcee525af3f774274fd08bcb37ddaac9911abb" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-72175/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "513983e702b45ecc0a589a4a295bea2c1c8806b2b18f8411f777b1a041b84109" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-72175/verification.json" "basis" 1728 "rationale" "The integrand is symmetric under exchange of coordinates, so the repeated integral on the transposed rectangle has the same value even though its bounds are assigned to opposite variables. Keep the original equality-truth request and correct the study solution to all three true equalities. The practice item receives a new all-three choice, key, field and answer identities; preserve original source choices, revealed key, actual Incorrect grade, uncertain solver decision, canonical source content and historical EDB versions."} {"question" "q-72175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{1+x^2y^2}$ is continuous on the rectangle $D=[0,\\sqrt2]\\times[-\\sqrt3,2]$. Fubini's theorem gives both I and II directly.\n\nIn III, the intervals are assigned to the opposite coordinate variables. Its region is the reflected rectangle $D^*=[-\\sqrt3,2]\\times[0,\\sqrt2]$, rather than $D$. Nevertheless, the integral has the same value: set $u=y$ and $v=x$, whose absolute Jacobian is $1$. Because $f(v,u)=f(u,v)$,\n\n$$\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{2}\\int_0^{\\sqrt2}\\sqrt{1+x^2y^2}\\,dy\\,dx\n=\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{2}\\int_0^{\\sqrt2}\\sqrt{1+u^2v^2}\\,du\\,dv\n=\\iint_D\\sqrt{1+x^2y^2}\\,dA.$$\n\nThus I, II, and III are all true. The original source's answer \"I and II only\" identifies the two expressions whose bounds directly match the stated coordinates, but omits the equal value in III due to symmetry. The study answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-72175/review.json" "file_sha256" "11e3ab7de29c35db8fbaa197e2dcee525af3f774274fd08bcb37ddaac9911abb" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-72175/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "513983e702b45ecc0a589a4a295bea2c1c8806b2b18f8411f777b1a041b84109" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-72175/verification.json" "basis" 1728 "rationale" "The integrand is symmetric under exchange of coordinates, so the repeated integral on the transposed rectangle has the same value even though its bounds are assigned to opposite variables. Keep the original equality-truth request and correct the study solution to all three true equalities. The practice item receives a new all-three choice, key, field and answer identities; preserve original source choices, revealed key, actual Incorrect grade, uncertain solver decision, canonical source content and historical EDB versions."} {"question" "q-72175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-72175/review.json" "file_sha256" "11e3ab7de29c35db8fbaa197e2dcee525af3f774274fd08bcb37ddaac9911abb" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-72175/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "513983e702b45ecc0a589a4a295bea2c1c8806b2b18f8411f777b1a041b84109" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-72175/verification.json" "basis" 1728 "rationale" "The integrand is symmetric under exchange of coordinates, so the repeated integral on the transposed rectangle has the same value even though its bounds are assigned to opposite variables. Keep the original equality-truth request and correct the study solution to all three true equalities. The practice item receives a new all-three choice, key, field and answer identities; preserve original source choices, revealed key, actual Incorrect grade, uncertain solver decision, canonical source content and historical EDB versions."} {"question" "q-72175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le y\\le 2}.$\n\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042681/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6bb21b64ba8165fa1ae7b4cddb17a4e99ed43b2fed49a60c9ff543a06bfb9da" "basis" 1727 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042681/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-72175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that:  \n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $0$ to $\\sqrt{2}$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $-\\sqrt{3}$ to $2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042681/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6bb21b64ba8165fa1ae7b4cddb17a4e99ed43b2fed49a60c9ff543a06bfb9da" "basis" 1727 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042681/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-72175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042681/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fdc9755692590715d69778410eed7c74fc2ff3ed1475976305ec45b51df123cc" "basis" 1727 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042681/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [] "usage" {"q-72175" []} "basis" 1728 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186328384 17592186328385 17592186328386 17592186328387 17592186328388 1759218632838